        "heading": "1 INTRODUCTION",
        "text": "Recent work (Brown et al., 2020; Artetxe et al., 2021; Rae et al., 2021) has demonstrated the potential of leveraging pretrained language models (PLMs) to perform zero-shot generalization. Zero-shot generalization enables PLMs to adapt to a variety of natural language processing (NLP) tasks without relying on any annotated data, which opens the possibility towards generic systems.\nPretrained models, such as GPT-3 (Brown et al., 2020), BERT (Devlin et al., 2018) and T5 (Raffel et al., 2019), can perform zero-shot inference on unseen test tasks by leveraging natural language prompts and formulating NLP tasks into language modeling tasks. More recent advances (Wei et al., 2021; Sanh et al., 2021) performed multi-task prompted training on PLMs and further enhanced the zero-shot performance to a large extent. Despite the substantial progress, few works have studied how multi-task prompted training boosts the zero-shot performance. The lack of understanding hinders further improvement of the field.\nTo this end, we take a further step to understand multi-task training for zero-shot generalization (1) by selecting only a small number of dominating training tasks and performing multi-task training and (2) by studying the characteristics of tasks with general transfer ability. The results reveal several interesting findings \u2014 First of all, only a small number of training tasks dominate the performance of zero-shot generalization. In other words, using only these dominating training tasks to perform multi-task training leads to good results while removing these dominating tasks would drastically hurt the zero-shot performance. Secondly, not all tasks are born equal, and some tasks show general transfer ability by providing widely useful knowledge. Moreover, dominant tasks with general transfer ability can be automatically detected using pairwise generalization results.\nIn addition, based on the findings, we propose an improved method, task reweighting , which improves multi-task training for zero-shot generalization to a large extent. Task reweighting first automatically identifies a set of key training tasks based on cross validation without peeking forward any test tasks, and then performs reweighting by upsampling dominating tasks or downsampling non-critical tasks, as shown in Figure 2. In this way, we build a better mixture of multi-task training sets to highlight those dominant tasks with better general transfer ability. Experiments show that\n1We will release our code upon acceptance.\ntask reweighting consistently outperforms the previous approach T0 (Sanh et al., 2021) across three different model scales and on test tasks of various types.\nTo sum up, our contributions are as follows.\n1. We conduct experiments to understand and reveal how multi-task training for zero-shot generalization works\u2014(1) Only a small number of training tasks dominate zero-shot generalization; (2) Some dominant tasks provide general transfer ability and can be detected using pairwise generalization results.\n2. We devise a novel method, task reweighting , to improve zero-shot generalization by (1) first automatically identifying key training tasks based on cross validation without observing any test tasks, and (2) reweighting training tasks using upsampling and downsampling strategies.\n3. Experiments show that our approach achieves new state-of-the-art results across various model scales and tasks."
